; WAITKEY: a keypress pause, Phase 4's first "pause and wait on a live
; event" feature. Genuinely halts the script and waits for a real
; keypress in the entry box, then outputs its name as a word -- so this
; example only makes sense run from the actual app, not the headless
; test driver, where it's a silent no-op (returns the empty word
; immediately) instead of waiting forever.

TO drive
  MAKE "key WAITKEY
  ; WHILE, not self-recursion: this can run for as many keypresses as
  ; it takes for the user to hit something else, and unlike a
  ; procedure calling itself, WHILE's loop body doesn't consume a real
  ; call stack level per iteration -- it's capped at 1,000,000
  ; iterations (MAX_WHILE_ITERATIONS), not the 200-deep recursion cap
  ; a self-recursive drive would hit after 200 keypresses.
  WHILE MEMBER? :key [Up Down Left Right] [
    IF :key = "Up [FD 20]
    IF :key = "Down [BK 20]
    IF :key = "Left [LT 15]
    IF :key = "Right [RT 15]
    MAKE "key WAITKEY
  ]
END

PRINT 'Press an arrow key to move the turtle -- any other key to stop.'
drive
PRINT "Stopped.
